<section>
<title>Examples</title>
<example>
<title>Calculating work for gas processes</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the work done on the gas for each of the three processes in
<xref ref="ex_PVexample">Example</xref> (see <xref ref="fig_PVexample">Figure</xref>).
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution:</em> Let's deal with each of the three processes in turn:
<ul>
<li><p><em>Process <m>1 \rightarrow 2</m>:</em> This is a constant-pressure
  expansion, so <m>p</m> can be taken out of the integral for work:
<md>
  <mrow>  W_\text{by}   =\mathstrut \amp  \int_{V_1}^{V_2}p\,dV</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  p\int_{V_1}^{V_2} dV</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  p (V_2 - V_1)</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  (100\,\text{kPa} )(6.0\,\text{L}  - 3.0\,\text{L} )</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  300\,\text{J} .</mrow>
</md>
The value of <m>W_\text{by}</m> is positive, which makes sense because
the gas is expanding (the volume is increasing).
A short-cut to the result would be to recognize 
that <m>W_\text{by}</m> 
is the area under the <m>p\,-V</m> curve, which in this case is 
a rectangle:
<men>
  W_\text{by}  = (100\,\text{kPa} )(6.0\,\text{L}  - 3.0\,\text{L} ) = 300\,\text{J} .
</men>
The work done on the gas is <m>W_\text{on}  = -W_\text{by}  = -300</m> J.</p></li>
<li><p><em>Process <m>2 \rightarrow 3</m>:</em> This is an isothermal
  compression, so <m>W_\text{on}  = -W_\text{by}  = -nRT\ln (V_3/V_2)</m>. We
  don't know how many moles of gas there are, but we could figure it
  out using the Ideal Gas Law.  <em>But we aren't going to do that</em>,
  because we can replace the entire product <m>nRT</m> with either <m>p_2V_2</m> 
or <m>p_3V_3</m>. So,
<md>
  <mrow>  W_\text{on}  =\mathstrut \amp  -p_2V_2\, \ln\left(\frac{V_3}{V_2}\right)</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  -(100 \,\text{kPa} )(6.0 L)\,\ln \left(\frac{3.0 L}{6.0 L}\right)</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  416 \,\text{J} .</mrow>
</md>
Note that this is a positive number, which makes sense
since it is a compression, and we know that <m>W_\text{on}</m> is positive for a
compression.  Note also that the work for the  process <m>2 \rightarrow 3</m> 
has a larger magnitude than that for process <m>1 \rightarrow 2</m>, which also
makes sense because there is a larger area under the curve <m>2 \rightarrow 3</m>
than under the curve <m>1 \rightarrow 2</m>.</p></li>
<li><p><em>Process <m>3 \rightarrow 4</m>:</em> This is a constant-volume process,
so <m>W_\text{on}  = 0</m>. This is also consistent with work as area under the 
curve, as the area under the curve for the process <m>3 \rightarrow 4</m> is
zero.</p></li>
</ul>

</p>
</statement>
</example>

<example>
<title>Adiabatic compression</title>
<statement>
<p>
A diatomic gas in a well-insulated container has an initial pressure,
temperature, and volume of <m>150\,\text{kPa}</m>, <m>300\,\text{K}</m>, and
<m>3.5\,\text{L}</m>. The gas is compressed to a volume of <m>0.5\,\text{L}</m>.
Calculate the pressure and temperature of the gas after the compression.
</p>

<p>
<em>Solution:</em> Since the container is insulated, <m>Q_\text{in} =0</m>,
and this is an adiabatic process. And since the gas is
composed of diatomic molecules at a temperature that isn't too high,
there are 5 degrees of freedom for the gas and <m>\gamma = \frac{f+2}{f} = 
\frac{7}{5}</m>.
Since this is an adiabatic process, <m>pV^{\gamma} = (\text{constant} )</m>, so
so <m>p_iV_i^\gamma = p_fV_f^\gamma</m>, which implies
<md>
  <mrow>  p_f =\mathstrut \amp  p_i\left(\frac{V_i}{V_f}\right)^\gamma</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  150 \,\text{kPa} \left(\frac{3.5 \,\text{L} }{0.5 \,\text{L} }\right)^{7/5}</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  2290\,\text{kPa} .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
To determine the temperature after the compression, remember that in
any adiabatic process <m>TV^{\gamma - 1} = \text{constant}</m>, so
<men>
  T_iV_i^{\gamma - 1} = T_fV_f^{\gamma - 1},
</men>
which implies that
<md>
  <mrow>  T_f =\mathstrut \amp  T_i\left(\frac{V_i}{V_f}\right)^{\gamma - 1}</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  (300 \,\text{K} )\left(\frac{3.5 \,\text{L} }{0.5 \,\text{L} }
                 \right)^{\frac{7}{5}-1}</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  653 \,\text{K} .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Note that the gas heats up, which is characteristic of any adiabatic
compression.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<example xml:id="ex_cycle2010tst3">
<title>A complete cycle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider a fixed amount of an ideal gas of diatomic molecules undergoing
the processes shown in <xref ref="fig_CycleExample">Figure</xref>. The gas starts at
point <em>A</em> in the diagram and expands along the path
<m>\textbf{A}\longrightarrow{B}</m> with no heat flowing into or out of the
gas.
</p>

<p>
(a) Calculate the pressure of the gas at point <em>B</em> after the
expansion. (b) Determine the net work done <em>on</em> the gas and the heat
flow <m>Q_\text{in}</m> in one complete cycle
(<m>\textbf{A}\longrightarrow{B}\longrightarrow{C}\longrightarrow{A}</m>).
</p>
<figure xml:id="fig_CycleExample" >
<caption>For <xref ref="ex_cycle2010tst3">Example</xref>.</caption>
<image width="56%" source="cycle" />
</figure>
<p>
<em>Solution:</em> (a) Since the process <m>\textbf{A} \rightarrow \textbf{B}</m> is
adiabatic, <m>pV^\gamma = (\text{constant} )</m> and
<m>p_AV_A^\gamma = p_BV_B^\gamma</m>
so
<men>
  p_B = p_A\left(\frac{V_A}{V_B}\right)^{7/5} = 100
  \,\text{kPa}  \times \left(\frac{1.0 \,\text{L} }{2.5 \,\text{L} }\right)^{7/5}
  = 27.7 \,\text{kPa} .
</men>
</p>

<p>
(b) We will calculate the work done on the gas for each of the three
processes and add up the results to get the net work. To figure out
the total heat flow <m>Q_\text{in}</m> we could do something similar <mdash/>
i.e., calculate <m>Q_\text{in}</m> for each process and then add them up. But
there is an easier way: since we know that <m>\Delta E_\text{therm}  = 0</m>
for the complete cycle, once we have determined the total <m>W_\text{on}</m>
for the complete cycle, we can use the First Law of Thermodynamics to
get <m>Q_\text{in}</m> for the complete cycle.
</p>

<p>
Let's start with process <m>\textbf{A} \rightarrow {B}</m>. This is an adiabatic
process, so <m>Q_\text{in}  = 0</m>. Calculating work is tricky for adiabatic
processes, so we'll find <m>\Delta E_\text{therm}</m> first and then use
the First Law to get <m>W_\text{on}</m>. This is a diatomic gas, so <m>f = 5</m>
and <m>\Delta E_\text{therm}  = \frac{f}{2}nR\Delta T</m>.
<m>\Delta E_\text{therm}</m> can be found
with the pressures and volumes given (see <xref ref="eq_Etherm_from_pV" />):
<md>
  <mrow>  \Delta E_\text{therm}  =\mathstrut \amp  \frac{f}{2}(p_BV_B - p_AV_A)</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  \frac{5}{2} \left(27.7 \,\text{kPa}  \times 
                   2.5 \,\text{L}  - 100 \,\text{kPa} \times 1.0 \,\text{L} \right)</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  -76.7 \,\text{J} .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
We can now find the work done on the gas via the First Law:
<md>
  <mrow>  W_\text{on}  =\mathstrut \amp  \Delta E_\text{therm}  - Q_\text{in}</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  -76.7 \,\text{J}  - 0</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  -76.7 \,\text{J} .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Process <m>\textbf{B} \rightarrow \textbf{C}</m> is a constant-volume process
(isochoric), so
<m>W_\text{on}  = 0</m>.
</p>

<p>
Process <m>\textbf{C} \rightarrow {A}</m> is a constant-pressure
process (isobaric), so
<md>
  <mrow>  W_\text{on}  =\mathstrut \amp  -p\,\Delta V</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  -p\, (V_A - V_C)</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  -100 \,\text{kPa} \, (1.0 \,\text{L}  - 2.5 \,\text{L} )</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  150 \,\text{J} .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
So, the net work <m>W_\text{on}</m> done on the gas for the complete cycle
<m>\textbf{A} \rightarrow \textbf{B} \rightarrow \textbf{C} \rightarrow \textbf{A}</m> is
<md>
  <mrow>  W_\text{on} ^\text{net}  
     =\mathstrut \amp  W_\text{on} ^{A\to B}+W_\text{on} ^{B\to C}+W_\text{on} ^{C\to A}</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  -76.7 \,\text{J}  + 0 + 150 \,\text{J}</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  73.3 \,\text{J} .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
As for the total heat flow <m>Q_\text{in}</m> for the entire cycle, we can use
the First Law:
<md>
  <mrow>  Q_\text{in}  =\mathstrut \amp  \Delta E_\text{therm}  - W_\text{on}</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  0 - 73.3 \,\text{J}</mrow>
  <mrow>  =\mathstrut \amp  -73.3 \,\text{J} .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
This means the net heat flow during the entire cycle is <m>73.3\,\text{J}</m>
flowing <em>out</em> of the gas.
</p>
</statement>
</example>
</section>
